  "transcript": "Most guys, when they wanna connect, reach for questions. Where are you from? What do you do? The problem? She's heard those a 100 times, and you're making her do all the interesting work. Better move. Instead of asking about her, tell her something about herself. You catch the one thing that stands out, and you riff on it. It goes in three beats. You notice it. You label it. Then you ask. If this is true, what else is true? Noticing is free. There's always one odd detail. She lines up the sugar packets before she'll even order, whatever it is. You don't ask why. You make up a why. That's the label. Not the real reason, a fun one. You're clearly the most organized person in here. The made up reason is the whole joke. You don't need anything more. If you want more, you exaggerate. Run that made up reason somewhere over the top. The key is to make it clearly a joke. Push it far enough that you obviously don't mean it. If the build could really happen, it's not a tease. It's just an observation. Anything can be funny. It just depends where you put the exaggeration. So push it. Two weeks in, you'll have color coded my closet and alphabetized my fridge, and I'll be too scared to move anything. You don't really mean it, and that's exactly why it works. And here's the part that feels backwards. The bigger you go, the safer you are. A small careful tease sounds like you might mean it. A huge ridiculous one is obviously a joke. So you're clearly fooling around, and she can tell. The willingness to say something that outlandish is attractive by itself. It reads as relaxed like you're not playing it safe. Wyatt Lance. It's about her. So it's not small talk and it could only have come from this exact moment. She can't hit you with, is that your line? Because it obviously isn't. From the first second, you're making the read, and she's measuring up to it. There's a bonus. Once you're playing in make believe, you can slip in the bolder stuff. A little romance, a little challenge, wrapped inside the joke where none of it's a big deal. And this reaches past dating. Being the person who catches the one real detail in a room and says something about it with a little edge makes you the most interesting person at the table anywhere. So here's the drill. A few minutes a day, grab the first odd thing. Make up a y, then push it till it's clearly a joke. Keep at it, and you stop hunting for the clever line. It just shows up the second you notice the thing. It becomes second nature. So put in the reps. That's how you stop interviewing people and become the one they're trying to impress.",
